\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The incidence matrix}
\date{2026-11-03}


\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, and let #{d} be the
\vocabk{ring-valued incidence map}{fdip-00AQ} of a finite carrier
#{\,(V,E,s,h)}. Choose orders of the vertex and edge indicators. Let
#{c_V:\mathcal F_R(V)\to R^{|V|}} and
#{c_E:\mathcal F_R(E)\to R^{|E|}} send a field to its ordered values.
The indicator expansion in \ref{fdip-00AC} gives their inverses, where
#{\delta_v} is the indicator field of a vertex #{v}. The
\newvocab{incidence matrix} represents #{c_Edc_V^{-1}}:
##{B_{ev}=(d\delta_v)(e)=\mathbf1_{v=h(e)}-\mathbf1_{v=s(e)}.}
Rows are indexed by edges and columns by vertices. A loop has a zero
row; reversing an edge negates its row. This extends the oriented-graph
sign convention of \citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}.
If coordinate reorderings are #{P_V,P_E}, then
#{B'=P_EBP_V^{-1}}, since both sides send #{c'_V(f)} to #{c'_E(df)}.}
